          "text": "{\"relation\":\"DIVERGENT\",\"consensus_summary\":\"The dominant consensus identifies lamp oil with the Holy Spirit—His indwelling, empowering, illuminating, and sustaining presence—while recognizing readiness, grace, faith, obedience, good works, or charity as alternative interpretations, especially in Matthew 25.\",\"book_summary\":\"The book identifies lamp oil not as the Holy Spirit Himself but as the tested fruit produced through the Spirit: accumulated evidence of faithful obedience that is consumed in giving visible light and example.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Lamp oil = the Holy Spirit or His sustaining spiritual presence and grace.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Lamp oil = stored fruit or evidence of Spirit-enabled faithful obedience, manifested as visible good works and example.\",\"core_relation\":\"CONTRADICTS\",\"extension_relation\":\"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book retains a causal connection to the Spirit but reclassifies the oil from the divine source to its humanly embodied product. It applies this especially to obedience, inwardly stored commandments or truth, and visible good works, cases that the consensus treats only as secondary or alternative readings.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"In the same lamp-oil symbolism, the dominant view says the oil is the Holy Spirit, whereas the book says it is the tested fruit and evidentiary result of the Spirit's work. Source and product are materially different referents, even though causally related.\",\"rationale\":\"This is not novel because the consensus explicitly records good works, obedience, genuine faith, grace, and preparedness as recognizable alternatives. Nevertheless, those are alternatives to the consensus core, not equivalent expressions of it. The book's own common-view note also distinguishes its interpretation from the customary Holy Spirit identification. Accepting the book's core therefore replaces the dominant referent rather than merely narrowing its range.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Matthew 5:16 directly connects visible light with good works, and the commandment, storage, wisdom, and obedience texts collectively support the book's product-of-obedience reading. However, no printed passage explicitly identifies lamp oil as tested or stored fruit of obedience; Exodus describes beaten oil literally, Zechariah strongly associates the lampstand complex with the Spirit, and Matthew 25 leaves the oil uninterpreted. The distinctive identification is therefore synthetic rather than directly stated.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
